---
import './benefits.css';
const ids = ['P7','P3','P7','P7','P40','P7','P3','P7'];
const seen = new Set();
const requests = ids.map(id => { const hit = seen.has(id); seen.add(id); return {id,hit}; });
---
<figure class="benefit-figure">
  <div class="figure-heading"><span class="figure-number">Figure 2</span>Eight requests, three source reads</div>
  <div class="scene">
    <div class="stats"><span><strong>5 / 8</strong> hits</span><span><strong>3</strong> source reads</span><span><strong>62.5%</strong> hit ratio</span></div>
    <ol class="trace" aria-label="Requests in arrival order">
      {requests.map((request,index) => <li class:list={{hit:request.hit}}><small>Request {index+1}</small><strong>{request.id}</strong><small>{request.hit?'Hit':'Miss → fill'}</small></li>)}
    </ol>
    <div class="flow"><div class="box"><strong>After request 1</strong>P7</div><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="box"><strong>After request 2</strong>P7, P3</div><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="box"><strong>After request 5</strong>P7, P3, P40</div></div>
    <p class="callout">P7 pays for one source read and avoids four more. P3 avoids one. P40 has not yet earned back its fill.</p>
  </div>
  <figcaption>An invented trace, starting empty. All requests use the same shop, locale and currency; P3 and P40 are other catalogue products. Requests finish in order, all fills succeed, and no entry expires, changes or is evicted during the trace. Each request asks for one card.</figcaption>
</figure>
